{"author":{"id":"ad87ffa09351c4e5a412ec8ee1546e86b314f33d6f28629bb4bcfe7b2290924d"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794303179,"nonce":"+SZa/FaIrGQDwlZG8RqDyyK474E=","title":"Previews have no sign-in: only the e2e API keys work","message":"A preview (https://skolia-pr-N.andyyu2004.workers.dev) has no Google client: Google only accepts exact redirect URIs, and each preview has its own host. So nothing that needs a signed-in user (cards, cloze cards, imports, reading positions) can be tried on a pull request's preview; only the end-to-end tests' API keys sign in there (infra/README.md, Previews).\n\nProposed fix: Better Auth's oAuthProxy plugin (in better-auth 1.7.12), through staging.\n- Previews start Google sign-in with staging's client, so Google redirects to https://staging.skolia.io/api/auth/callback/google, which is registered. Staging encrypts the profile (60 s max age) and redirects to the preview's /api/auth/callback/google/oauth-proxy, which creates the user and session in the preview's own D1. No change in the Google console.\n- Code: oAuthProxy({ productionURL: staging, secret }) on staging and previews (not production); staging trusts https://skolia-pr-*.andyyu2004.workers.dev as origins.\n- Secrets: staging's GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET in the preview GitHub environment, and a shared OAUTH_PROXY_SECRET on staging and previews (each environment's BETTER_AUTH_SECRET differs), passed by just deploy-web.\n\nTo decide: previews are public and imports aren't capped since #52, so signing in on a preview lets anyone with a Google account run its containers. Maybe allow only listed emails on previews. Staging being down would also stop sign-in on previews.","files":null}]}